---

<Doc
  tocEntries={tocEntries}
  currentSlug="fromsubstack"
  title="fromsubstack"
  section="@plugdash/import"
  description="Move a Substack archive into EmDash with one command."
>
  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>@plugdash/import</code> CLI reads your Substack export and
    creates the entries on your EmDash site over the REST API: body as
    Portable Text, images uploaded to the media library, tags, SEO fields
    and the original dates. You run it from your laptop, so nothing is
    added to your site and nothing is registered in
    <code>astro.config.mjs</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The parser lives in <code>@plugdash/fromsubstack</code>, which the CLI uses. That
    package also registers an EmDash import source, but EmDash's admin
    import screen lists only WordPress sources, so the CLI is the way to
    run the import.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <InstallCommand command={plugin.installCommand} />
  <p>Or run it once without installing: <code>pnpm dlx @plugdash/import --help</code>. Needs Node 20 or newer.</p>

  <h2 id="get-an-api-token">Get an API token</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In your site's admin, open <strong>Settings &gt; API Tokens</strong>.</li>
    <li>Create a token with the scopes <code>content:read</code>, <code>content:write</code>, <code>media:read</code>, <code>media:write</code> and <code>schema:read</code>.</li>
    <li>Copy it. It starts with <code>ec_pat_</code> and is shown once.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Pass it as <code>--token</code>, or set <code>EMDASH_TOKEN</code> and <code>EMDASH_URL</code> to keep it out of your shell history.</p>

  <h2 id="export">Export from Substack</h2>
  <p>In Substack, go to <strong>Settings &gt; Exports &gt; Create new export</strong>. You get a <code>.zip</code> with <code>posts.csv</code> and one HTML file per post.</p>

  <h2 id="run-the-import">Run the import</h2>
  <Code language="bash" code={`# see what would happen, write nothing
npx plugdash-import substack ./substack-export.zip --url https://your-site.com --token ec_pat_... --dry-run

# import everything as drafts
npx plugdash-import substack ./substack-export.zip --url https://your-site.com --token ec_pat_...`} />
  <ul>
    <li>Images are downloaded from Substack's CDN, so they need no extra flag.</li>
    <li>Podcast episodes are skipped.</li>
    <li>Paid posts import as drafts by default, even with <code>--publish</code>, so you can review them. Pass <code>--paid-posts-as publish</code> to publish them too.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Reads the export and prints how many posts, pages and tags it found, and where each goes.</li>
    <li>Checks that the target collection has a <code>title</code> field and a Portable Text body field (<code>content</code>, or <code>--field</code>). If not, it stops before writing anything. <code>--dry-run</code> stops here.</li>
    <li>Skips any entry whose slug already exists.</li>
    <li>Uploads each image once and points the body and featured image at the media library copy.</li>
    <li>Creates the entry. With <code>--publish</code>, publishes entries that were published at the source; without it, everything is a draft.</li>
    <li>Prints a summary and exits with code 1 if any entry failed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Running it twice is safe: the second run skips what the first created, so a run that failed halfway can be finished by running it again.</p>

  <h2 id="known-limitations">Known limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Authors are not imported. Entries are owned by the token's user.</li>
    <li>Tags need a <code>tag</code> taxonomy on the target collection (the blog template has one). Without it, posts import without tags and a warning.</li>
    <li>An <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> is dropped. Images inside <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code> work.</li>
    <li>Subscribers, comments and paid status are not imported. Once published, every entry is public.</li>
  </ul>

  <PluginReference plugin={plugin} />

  <h2 id="for-agents">For agents</h2>
  <p>
    To move a Substack site into EmDash, do not register a plugin. Ask
    the user for the export file and an API token with the five scopes
    above, then run
    <code>npx plugdash-import substack &lt;file&gt; --url &lt;site&gt; --token &lt;token&gt; --dry-run</code>.
    If the dry run reports a missing collection or field, fix the schema
    or pass <code>--collection</code> / <code>--field</code>. Then run the
    same command without <code>--dry-run</code>, and check the drafts in
    the admin. Data stored: the imported entries and media. The CLI keeps
    no state of its own.
  </p>
</Doc>
